<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hòa Hảo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hoa Hao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hòa Hảo is a Vietnamese Buddhist reform movement founded in 1939 by Huỳnh Phú Sổ in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> village of Hòa Hảo. It stresses simple lay devotion over temples and clergy and quickly won a mass peasant following. In the 1940s and 1950s it had its own armed forces, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> and later Ngô Đình Diệm's government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoa-hao.jpg" alt="Hòa Hảo" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ban Trị sự Trung ương Giáo hội Phật giáo Hòa Hảo (Hòa Hảo Buddhist Sangha's Central Administrative Committee) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_of_H%C3%B2a_H%E1%BA%A3o.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hòa Hảo grew from the Miền Tây region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a>, a frontier society where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and spirit cults mixed and the French imposed their rule in 1867. It claims to rest on the teaching of Phật Thầy Tây An (1807–1856), the Bửu Sơn Kỳ Hương tradition, whose prophecies of the fall of Nguyễn Vietnam to Western powers helped inspire rebellions in 1875 and 1913. Its founder, Huỳnh Phú Sổ, came from a Roman Catholic family of smallholders and is regarded as a saint by devotees. Fearing revolt, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-decoux">Jean Decoux</a> had him imprisoned in Bạc Liêu as a lunatic, and in 1942 the French exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The movement flourished under the Japanese occupation and became a militant nationalist force with a private army. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender it joined the front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a>, then clashed with the Việt Minh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caodaism">Cao Đài</a>. The Việt Minh kidnapped and executed Sổ in 1947, and the movement split into factions whose leaders carried out public burnings, beheadings and mass executions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmers</a> and Vietnamese, and drowned Việt Minh sympathisers. The French funded its forces, arming some 20,000 men against the Việt Minh. After 1954 it fought Ngô Đình Diệm's government, and from the mid-1960s, left to rule the Miền Tây in return for recognising South Vietnam, it waged war on the Việt Cộng until the Fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Hòa Hảo is described either as a syncretistic folk religion or as a Buddhist sect, blending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> with ancestor worship, animistic rites, Confucian ideas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> tradition. It avoids elaborate rituals and temples, keeps no monastic order and teaches practice at home, holding that each believer can commune directly with the Buddha and that inner faith matters more than rites. Daily observance is four prayers, and funerals are simple, which kept the cost of religion low for peasants and earned it the name poor man's Buddhism. Disbanded after 1975, the faith was officially recognised by the Vietnamese state in 1999, and most of its followers live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hòa Hảo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 3 and 4 million people were Buddhists, the Cao Đài and <b>Hòa Hảo</b> religions had between 2.5 and 3 million followers, and about 10% of the population was Catholic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious movements such as Cao Dai and <b>Hoa Hao</b> arose.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cochinchina">French Cochinchina</a></li><li><blockquote>Its authority was challenged by the Viet Minh in the countryside and by armed religious sects, the Cao Dai and <b>Hoa Hao</b>, and the Binh Xuyen gang in Saigon.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a></li></ul></section>
